  of micrornas are stored in exosomes or  protein complexes and so you need a  strong lysis in order to  release the micrornas from that and so  the way to do that is through  organic solvents for example trisol or a  mechanical or enzymatic lysis  such as with proteinase k you also want  to try to remove  as much as dna as possible to enrich the  concentration of your micro rna as much  as possible to be able to  detect you know very low occurring  as well as the higher occurring targets  and so there's several methods that will  provide the strong license step  as well as dna removal the first is your  organic base this  is going to be your trisol combined with  an ethanol  cleanup or a column cleanup at the end  there's your column-based systems for  example um like the mirvanna paris  these are more um but again these do  still require kind of more organic up  front  then finally you have your high  throughput high throughput solutions  your magnetic bead based  this is generally going to use your  enzymatic treatments  in order to laser sample so you are able  to easily  you know process a high throughput or a  large number of samples at one time  um you know however as  rna purification even if you have a good  method there is still some donor to  donor variation to consider which can  affect your ability to recover  um you know as much rna as possible from  your sample  and so you want to make sure that your  purification method or extraction method  is performing optimally for all your  samples and a good way to do this is by  using an extraction control to monitor  recovery um you know it's it's  it's kind of a good um check or a good  addition um to use with your  normalization  method to really give you confidence  that the expression pattern differences  you see  are true and real  and so unfortunately for micro rna for  message rna you can use a lot of times  endogenous  messenger rna um it's pretty stable  between donors azure control but for  micro rna this isn't  as useful or as consistent  and so for microrna we  try to use with more synthetic type of  samples and these include kind of  what i've been using is our nirvana  microrna mimics they're modified in  order to protect the rna  from anything in the sample that could  degrade it  and so it makes it ideal for use of  serum plasma so you can spike it into  your sample  and then monitor it all the way through  to the pcr detection  and so here's just an example of a  workflow  you can see i've used two c elegans  micro rna and spiked them into  human serum and plasma and then  extracted them with our magmax mirvana  kit  along with any microrna in the sample  and then performed pcr using our  attackman advanced microrna  workflow and so what you can see here  is looking at five different donors for  both serum plasma  if you look at the specific synthetic  microrna targets  the detection pattern is very consistent  from donor to donor so you're not seeing  a lot of effect from the donor  especially for the  um the lin 45p  the other nice thing is if you actually  um  you know here are two samples with the  spike in and then a sample with no spike  in and you can see  here we're looking at mir-16 targets  there is no effect on the expression  pattern of mere 16  um in the serum plasma by using the  spike in um  and so you're able to see good  extraction and detection  um all the way around so extraction  control is a good addition  to make sure your rna purification is  working  and then to make sure that any  normalization  also will work as well so at this point  i'm actually going to hand it off to my  colleague karita  to talk about the last two topics  all right emily thank you so much for  that nice overview  of sample handling purification  and now we will uh move on to our next  topic which is  how what are the solutions available to  um  quantify uh rna as well as uh  normalization techniques  so uh with respect to um the tools that  are available for quantification  we have two unique chemistries that are  offered  um by the taqman chemistry um the first  is the taqman microarray assays  these are assays which are designed  to specifically detect micrornas from  samples  and they come with a micrornas specific  reverse  transcription step followed by micrornas  specific  real-time detection of the target of  interest  the other chemistry that is uh offered  is also trackman based however this is  based on universal reverse transcription  wherein all uh micrornas that are  present in a sample  are reverse transcribed at the same time  for this technology we have poly  adenylation happening at the three prime  end and  adapter ligation at the five prime end  uh following cdna synthesis uh micrornas  of interest can be detected using  pacman advanced microrna assays so you  can see that  the two uh chemistries complement each  other very well  and can be used for specific  applications  to to get a broader coverage  both these chemistries are available in  four different formats  again to facilitate wide array of  research  um single tube assays as you can see  provide the maximum flexibility  um although these are more suitable for  load throughput  analysis and applications such as  verification  tachman array plates are  come with pre-spotted assays both  legacy and advanced assays more suitable  for  small to medium throughput analysis and  you can investigate anywhere from 96 to  384  samples ideal for verification and  discovery  contact array cards are also pre-spotted  um however these are probably more  suitable for discovery and  profiling studies and enable the  customer to  investigate up to eight different  samples at the same time our highest  throughput uh  platform is the open array arrays  place where um both chemistries  could be pre-spotted and they allow you  to  investigate up to 48 different samples  so those are the different uh  chemistries and  formats that are available for  quantification  with that i'm going to move on to  normalizing  rna qpcr data  and like most other micro  rna expression analysis micro rna  analysis is also  very critical and the reason behind that  is because  any variability that you observe in your  samples  should be a reflection of the biology  behind it  and hence normalization is important to  remove  um the differences that come in because  of  um you know samples uh  because of technical variability and  things like that  and so normalization is extremely  critical  micro rna qpcr  data is typically normalized um in three  different ways  and uh to make these different  strategies  more understandable i will go through  some of the case studies  um and we'll go delve a little deeper  into what each of these  strategies brings in so first  looking at global mean normalization um  to demonstrate global  global mean normalization which is often  used  to normalize large sets of data  in which several hundred or several  thousand targets  are investigated and how does this work  well in global mean normalization we  look  at targets that are common to all  samples in a study  identify the cq mean of all of these  targets  and then use the cq for cq median  and use the cq median as a normalization  factor  for all targets in the sample study  so that's the um the the basis of  global mean normalization and uh  for uh demonstrating that what we did  was we used  a small sample set of uh matched normal  and diseased  plasma from donors who were previously  identified  with chronic lymphocytic leukemia  samples were extracted stored  sorry stored and extracted as described  by emily in the few  earlier slides they were reversed  transcribed with the attackman advanced  micro rna serina synthesis kit and then  processed  on the taqman open array human advanced  microrna panel  these arrays were run on the quant  studio 12k  flex system and data from these was  analyzed  on the thermofisher connect using  our queue app and to demonstrate global  normalization  we used global normalization as  the option for data analysis what did we  find  uh we found that when data was analyzed  and we  looked at clustering of  differential gene expression between the  four samples that we looked at  um the data clustered by  the phenotype of the sample so the  disease samples were clustered together  while the normal samples were clustered  together which was what was expected  for the set of samples  all right so that's one way of  normalizing data  the other a very uh widely used  um strategy to normalize uh  gene expression data is the use of  endogenous or reference streams  now the challenge with um finding  reference genes for microscopy  is that most micrornas are  known to be variably expressed in in  tissues and biofluids and hence it's  very difficult to find  micrornas that are uniformly expressed  in any tissue  and especially in serum and plasma  historically  snow rnas and u6 rnas were used  as reference genes however because  the biogenesis of snow rnase um or u670  is  so different from that of micrornas  based on myki guidelines we are moving  away from that recommendation  um also my key guidelines suggest that  while normalizing  expression data using endogenous control  you should at least have  two or three different reference genes  which are stably expressed in a set of  samples  so because of these challenges one of  the first things  we recommend customers to do is to  identify  those reference genes or normalization  genes in their set of samples  and for that we have a very uh nicely  defined workflow which is a systematic  way of identifying these genes  um and we recommend using a spike in  control like the one that emily  mentioned earlier in the slides  in this case study we use the c elegans  mir 39 3p  as a spike in control into plasma  samples  and um rna was extracted uh using the  magmax marijuana kit followed by  cdna synthesis and qpcr this time  on the human endogenous control cards  the control cards consist of 30 unique  micrornas that are known to be  stably expressed in various samples  including serum and plasma  and this information is based on  literature so  we ran these cards on the  quantstudio ps7 system and analyze the  data on thermo fischer connect like  previously  this case study resulted in  identification of three micrornas in our  samples which were found to be  uniquely stable so these were  um hsa mirror 1983p hsa mirror 451a  and hsa merc 939 5b  once we had identified these  three genes as stably expressed in our  samples we use them as our reference  streams  in the sample set that was earlier run  through  the open array platform and this time  instead of choosing  global mean normalization for data  analysis  we chose endogenous controls and picked  the three genes  which were earlier identified as our  normalizing  um genes and what did we find  well we found that the uh differential  expression  um that we obtained from um  using global mean normalization and that  obtained using reference genes were was  remarkably concordant so very similar  kind of data you observe although keep  in mind that it is important that both  these strategies for normalization have  a place in its own  so global mean normalization is  typically recommended for  large high throughput studies and  reference genes could be used  both for high throughput studies and for  smaller sets of studies  but it's critical to identify the right  reference genes in  your set of samples  although i used open array to  demonstrate  the these different strategies i just  want to point out  that all of the panels that are  available in the open  array format are also available in the  taskman array  card format and here is a data set which  shows that the differential gene  expression data  you get from one format versus the other  in this case stack memory cards versus  open array  is very similar and they could be used  based on  particular application  with that a quick review of what we  learned today  we learned that it was important to  collect samples and store them  you know as recommended as emily went  through  the recommendation we also learned that  there's  many different um two different  chemistries that are available in  in many different formats which could be  custom used for different applications  and that data normalization of micro rna  qpcr data is  extremely critical and it's important to  choose the right strategy with that  we will now take some questions  thank you emily sarita for the excellent  talk  we will now move into the q live q and a  portions of the presentations  so we have several questions coming in  uh the first questions would be to emily  the extraction control can help to a  certain  extent but control rna is different from  the sample  including their micro environment  besides the extraction control are there  other ways of controlling the quality of  extractions  um so yeah to use that question um  there are other ways um generally this  will be  i'm assuming the question means a  testing for um  optimal lysis of the cells um  in the sample in order to get to the  nucleic acid um the easiest way are to  do actual intact cell  um you know to measure the ability to  recover  rna and to lyse  the spiking controls we discuss here are  more meant for normalization  [Music]  and to look specifically just at the rna  recovery  um not necessarily at the lysis  efficiency  thank you emily the second question for  you would be which method is the best  for rna extraction  for high purity yield  um so really that's going to depend on  kind of what throughput you need and the  sample type you're working with  you know our extraction methods both are  our high-throughput bead-based method  the magmax systems as well as our column  purcation like the purelink or the  standard nirvana both generate  a very high pure rna and high yield of  samples  so really both will work it just depends  on  you know which better fit your  throughput needs  thank you emily um so the next question  i would like to direct you harita  your question would be besides tag men  are they other chemistry for microrna  analysis by real-time qpcr  like any other platform for real-time  rt pcr that does not use pro  um so the two chemistries that we offer  for  both for rtq pcr are  based on the tax and probes  we do have other solutions which are  not rtqpcr based such as the microarray  assays the detection of microrna  but for rtq pcr we only have  tachman based assays  the second question for harita is can  the normalization be itself  the source of error in some cases  and how do you decide what kind of  normalization to use  yes normalization is actually a very key  component of  how the rtq pcr experiments  um you know can be analyzed and  the choice of normalization can skew the  data  any which way so it is very very  important to choose the right  methods for normalization and  i think we went through a couple of  different  ways it really depends upon your sample  and you know the kind of uh targets that  are uh stably expressed so if there is a  target that is stably expressed then  um they can be used as a normalizing  agent  otherwise um as we went through in the  presentation  spiking controls are a good choice um  or uh using global normalization so the  three options available and  um the kind of samples and the  availability of different  the presence of different uh target  targets which are normally expressed in  the samples will  decide which which methodology to choose  okay the last question we have can you  use more than one kind of normalization  or combined with certain essay  so uh you could  probably normalize your data two  different ways and  and make sure that you know the results  are  similar um they should if if the  technique is correct then  as i had shown in in my slides uh  no matter which way you normalize you  should still get a  very similar result the the  expression pattern should be similar  you could but i don't think there is a  way to really use  uh two different ways in a same  set of experiments like together it  might be difficult to do  i i'm not aware of any uh analysis  software which will  help you do that but you can definitely  do it two ways  thank you harita so with this  i would like to thank you for all the  questions and the speaker for your time  to be here today  as a final reminder any questions that  were submitted and were not answered  today by our speaker will be addressed  by via email this ends the qc in  genomics sessions  we will now take a 15-minute break  before we start the next session  in other tracks so you can have a coffee  and visit our booth  learn about some very exciting  promotions that is made available to you  where you can ask questions to our  thermal fusion representative  please also participate the scavenger  hunting games  look for our icons in the environment to  win prizes  there will be a pop-up survey appearing  in a minute  please help us to complete the survey  and thank you  and speak to you again in 15 minutes

